      "sequence_position": 9,
      "problem": "Let $n$ be an integer. Prove that $n$ is odd provided that ${n}^{2}-5n+5$ is even.\n\nThe contrapositive form of the given statement is as follows:\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\,⇒\\,{{field-2}}\n$$\n\nWe will prove this statement instead of the original one.\n\nIf $n$ is {{field-3}}, then $n=$ {{field-4}} for some integer $a.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-5n+5 & ={{field-5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $b=$ {{field-6}}. Since $a$ is {{field-7}}, we have that $b$ is {{field-8}}.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-5n+5 & ={{field-9}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich {{field-10}} a multiple of $2.$ Therefore ${n}^{2}-5n+5$ is {{field-11}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n\n$$\n{n}^{2}-5n+5\\text{ is even}\\,⇒\\,n\\text{ is odd}\n$$\n\nTo prove $P⇒Q,$ it suffices to prove the contrapositive $¬Q⇒¬P.$ So, let's write down the contrapositive of our statement.\n\nThe contrapositive form of the given statement is as follows:\n\n$$\nn\\text{ is even}\\,⇒\\,{n}^{2}-5n+5\\text{ is odd}\n$$\n\nWe'll prove this statement instead of the original one.\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is even.\n\nIf $n$ is even, then $n=2a$ for some integer $a.$\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a$ into the expression ${n}^{2}-5n+5$ and show it is odd by writing it as a multiple of $2,$ plus $1.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-5n+5 & =(2a)^{2}-5(2a)+5 \\\\  & =4{a}^{2}-10a+5 \\\\  & =(4{a}^{2}-10a+4)+1 \\\\  & =2{{(2{a}^{2}-5a+2)}_{⏟}}_{b}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we define another integer $b=2{a}^{2}-5a+2$ (highlighted above), we have that ${n}^{2}-5n+5=2b+1,$ which shows that ${n}^{2}-5n+5$ is indeed one more than a multiple of $2.$\n\nLet $b=2{a}^{2}-5a+2.$ Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-5n+5 & =2{{(2{a}^{2}-5a+2)}_{⏟}}_{b}+1 \\\\  & =2b+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is one larger than a multiple of $2.$\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, ${n}^{2}-5n+5$ is odd.",
